{
  "voice_id": "q0IMILNRPxOgtBTS4taI",
  "model_id": "eleven_v4_turbo",
  "beats": [
    "fk",
    "parent",
    "many",
    "defend",
    "ninety",
    "ri-in",
    "delete",
    "restrict",
    "cascade",
    "setnull",
    "choose",
    "update",
    "next"
  ],
  "text": "An order belongs to a customer, so it stores the customer's key, in customer ID. That's a foreign key. The table it points at is the parent. The one holding the pointer is the child. It lives on the many side, and it copies nothing else. Just the ID.\n\nPostgres checks that line. An order for customer one goes right in. Customer ninety-nine doesn't exist, so that order's rejected. It would've been an orphan, a child whose parent isn't there. But what happens when a parent leaves?\n\nReferential integrity means every pointer points at something real. So deleting a parent forces a choice. Restrict blocks it. Ada Co still has an invoice, so the account stays. Cascade takes the children along. Delete the spam post, and BOTH its comments go too. And set null keeps the child but clears the pointer. Sam leaves, and his ticket stays, assigned to no one.\n\nSo which? Ask what the child means without its parent. Nothing? Cascade. Can it stand alone? Set null. A record you must keep? Restrict. Changing a parent's key asks the same question... one more reason to pick a key that never changes. And with keys to point with, you can build every shape a relationship takes.",
